{
  "id": "congenital_adrenal_hyperplasia",
  "name": "Congenital Adrenal Hyperplasia",
  "aliases": [
    "CAH",
    "21-hydroxylase deficiency",
    "adrenogenital syndrome"
  ],
  "class": "non_infective",
  "system": "Endocrine",
  "specialty": "Paediatrics",
  "referenceOnly": true,
  "reference": {
    "source": "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics",
    "sourceEdition": "22e",
    "pages": null,
    "clinicalPearls": [
      "A group of autosomal recessive disorders of adrenal steroid synthesis, the great majority caused by 21-hydroxylase deficiency.",
      "The classic salt-wasting form presents in the first weeks of life with vomiting, poor feeding, weight loss, dehydration, and shock from combined cortisol and aldosterone deficiency.",
      "Female infants with the classic form often show virilisation of the external genitalia (ambiguous genitalia), whereas affected males may appear normal at birth and are at particular risk of unrecognised salt-wasting crisis.",
      "Characteristic biochemistry includes hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, and metabolic acidosis in the salt-wasting crisis, with markedly elevated 17-hydroxyprogesterone.",
      "A non-classic (late-onset) form presents later with signs of androgen excess such as premature pubarche, hirsutism, or menstrual irregularity.",
      "Newborn screening measuring 17-hydroxyprogesterone allows early detection before a life-threatening crisis.",
      "Treatment principle is glucocorticoid replacement to correct cortisol deficiency and suppress excess adrenal androgen, with mineralocorticoid replacement (and salt supplementation in infancy) for salt-wasting forms.",
      "Additional stress steroid cover is essential during illness, surgery, or major stress to prevent adrenal crisis.",
      "Management of affected girls includes multidisciplinary care regarding virilised genitalia, and genetic counselling is important for families."
    ],
    "pathophysiology": "A deficiency of an enzyme in the adrenal cortisol synthesis pathway, most often 21-hydroxylase, reduces cortisol production. The resulting loss of negative feedback drives increased ACTH secretion, which stimulates adrenal hyperplasia and diverts steroid precursors into the androgen pathway, causing androgen excess and virilisation. In the severe salt-wasting form, aldosterone synthesis is also impaired, leading to sodium loss, hyperkalaemia, and volume depletion, while accumulation of the precursor 17-hydroxyprogesterone serves as a diagnostic marker.",
    "additionalDifferentials": [
      "Other causes of ambiguous genitalia and disorders of sex development",
      "Pyloric stenosis or sepsis in a vomiting, dehydrated neonate",
      "Other forms of primary adrenal insufficiency",
      "Rarer enzyme defects (11-beta-hydroxylase, 3-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase deficiency)",
      "Precocious puberty from other causes",
      "Polycystic ovary syndrome (for non-classic presentations)"
    ],
    "additionalInvestigations": [
      "Serum 17-hydroxyprogesterone (baseline and, if needed, after ACTH stimulation)",
      "Serum electrolytes, glucose, and acid-base status",
      "Plasma renin activity and aldosterone",
      "Adrenal androgens (androstenedione, testosterone)",
      "Karyotype and pelvic ultrasound in ambiguous genitalia",
      "Genetic analysis of the CYP21A2 gene"
    ],
    "redFlags": [
      "Salt-wasting adrenal crisis with dehydration, hypotension, or shock",
      "Severe hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia",
      "Hypoglycaemia",
      "Vomiting and poor feeding in a neonate, particularly a male infant",
      "Intercurrent illness or surgery without adequate stress steroid cover"
    ],
    "prognosis": "With early diagnosis, hormone replacement, and stress dosing, children generally grow and develop well; risks include life-threatening adrenal crises, effects of over- or under-treatment on growth and puberty, and fertility and psychosocial issues in classic disease.",
    "pitfalls": [
      "Missing salt-wasting crisis in a male infant with normal genitalia",
      "Failing to provide stress steroid cover during illness or surgery",
      "Over-treating with glucocorticoids, impairing growth, or under-treating, allowing androgen excess",
      "Misattributing a neonatal collapse to sepsis or pyloric stenosis without checking electrolytes and adrenal steroids"
    ],
    "references": [
      "Nelson Textbook of Pediatrics, 22e"
    ],
    "crossLinks": [],
    "management": [
      "Replace cortisol with glucocorticoid therapy (hydrocortisone in children) to relieve deficiency and suppress ACTH-driven adrenal androgen excess",
      "Add mineralocorticoid replacement with fludrocortisone plus sodium chloride supplementation in the salt-wasting form",
      "Treat salt-wasting adrenal crisis urgently with intravenous saline, glucose, parenteral hydrocortisone and correction of hyperkalaemia",
      "Educate on sick-day rules, provide an emergency steroid card and injectable hydrocortisone, and increase glucocorticoid cover during illness and surgery",
      "Monitor growth, bone age and androgen markers such as 17-hydroxyprogesterone and androstenedione to balance over- and under-treatment",
      "Coordinate multidisciplinary care with paediatric endocrinology, surgery for genital reconstruction where indicated, and psychological support",
      "Offer genetic counselling and family screening given autosomal recessive CYP21A2 inheritance"
    ]
  },
